WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
HTML attributes require different care from HTML body text.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.320
In a quoted attribute, a quote character can break out of the attribute if it is not encoded correctly.

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.940
In an unquoted attribute, spaces and additional characters can create even more ambiguity.

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:24.000
The safest pattern is to use quoted attributes, framework-supported binding mechanisms, and attribute-aware encoding.

5
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.740
Avoid building attribute strings manually when the framework can set attributes safely.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.060
URL output is also context-specific.

7
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.400
A full URL, a path segment, a query parameter, and a fragment are not identical contexts.

8
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:45.300
Safe URL construction should use library functions that understand URL components rather than string concatenation.

9
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.080
Validation matters too.

10
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.020
Encoding a dangerous scheme does not necessarily make a navigation safe.

11
00:00:51.120 --> 00:01:02.560
Check allowed schemes such as HTTPS where appropriate, constrain destinations, and be careful with redirects, links, callback URLs, and user-controlled return paths.

12
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.560
CSS contexts are easy to underestimate.

13
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:13.500
Dynamic CSS values can interact with browser parsing, external references, and injection patterns in surprising ways.

14
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:20.240
Prefer static styles and class toggles over inserting user-controlled values into style blocks or style attributes.

15
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:29.540
When dynamic styling is necessary, constrain values to a narrow allowlist such as known class names, colors, lengths, or tokens.

16
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:36.360
Encoding is important, but the best defense is often to avoid dynamic CSS from untrusted data altogether.
